2. Threads and Runnable equivalents

Plain callable or lambda

A C++ thread starts immediately when its  std::thread  object is constructed.

#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

void runTask() {
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        std::cout << "Thread " << std::this_thread::get_id()
                  << " running: " << i << '\n';

        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(500));
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t1(runTask);
    std::thread t2(runTask);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

Always  join()  or  detach()  a  std::thread . Destroying a joinable  std::thread  calls  std::terminate() .

Runnable-style callable object

#include <iostream>
#include <thread>

class MyRunnable {
public:
    void operator()() const {
        std::cout << "Running on " << std::this_thread::get_id() << '\n';
    }
};

int main() {
    MyRunnable task;

    std::thread t1(task);
    std::thread t2(task);

    t1.join();
    t2.join();
}

 operator()  makes an object callable. Lambdas are usually preferred:

std::thread t([] {
    std::cout << "Task running\n";
});

Passing shared state

Pass references explicitly with  std::ref :

#include <functional>
#include <thread>

void increment(int& value) {
    ++value;
}

int main() {
    int value = 0;
    std::thread t(increment, std::ref(value));
    t.join();
}

This example still requires synchronization if multiple threads access  value .

