4. Exceptions inside threads

An uncaught exception escaping a C++ thread function calls  std::terminate() , potentially ending the entire process.

std::thread worker([] {
    try {
        throw std::runtime_error("failure");
    } catch (const std::exception& error) {
        std::cerr << error.what() << '\n';
    }
});
worker.join();

To propagate an exception to another thread, use  std::future :

#include <future>
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

int main() {
    auto result = std::async(std::launch::async, []() -> int {
        throw std::runtime_error("task failed");
    });

    try {
        std::cout << result.get();
    } catch (const std::exception& error) {
        std::cout << "Caught: " << error.what() << '\n';
    }
}

 get()  rethrows the original exception.

────────────────────

5. Callable and Future

A C++ callable can return any type and throw exceptions.

#include <chrono>
#include <future>
#include <string>
#include <thread>

std::string callable(std::string name) {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(500));
    return "Completed " + name;
}

int main() {
    auto future1 =
        std::async(std::launch::async, callable, "Task 1");

    auto future2 =
        std::async(std::launch::async, callable, "Task 2");

    std::cout << future1.get() << '\n';
    std::cout << future2.get() << '\n';
}

Always specify  std::launch::async  when asynchronous execution is required. Without it, an implementation may defer execution until  get() .

