6. Cooperative interruption with  std::jthread 

C++ does not safely force-stop threads. C++20 provides cooperative cancellation.

#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>
#include <stop_token>
#include <thread>

void worker(std::stop_token token) {
    while (!token.stop_requested()) {
        std::cout << "Working...\n";
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(200));
    }

    std::cout << "Stopping cleanly\n";
}

int main() {
    std::jthread thread(worker);

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));
    thread.request_stop();
}

 std::jthread  automatically joins when destroyed.

Unlike Java interruption, ordinary  sleep_for()  and  condition_variable::wait()  do not automatically throw an interruption exception.

