7. Synchronization

Synchronized method equivalent

#include <mutex>

class Counter {
    int count_ = 0;
    mutable std::mutex mutex_;

public:
    void increment() {
        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);
        ++count_;
    }

    int getCount() const {
        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);
        return count_;
    }
};

 std::lock_guard  acquires the mutex in its constructor and releases it automatically when the scope ends.

Synchronized block equivalent

void increment() {
    doPreprocessing();

    {
        std::lock_guard lock(mutex_);
        ++count_;
    }

    doPostprocessing();
}

Only the inner scope is protected.

Protect reads too

If one thread writes  count_ , unsynchronized reads of  count_  are a data race. Both reads and writes must use the same mutex or an atomic variable.

