8.  volatile  versus  std::atomic 

Java  volatile  and C++  volatile  are not equivalent.

C++  volatile  does not provide thread safety, atomicity, or inter-thread visibility guarantees. It is primarily intended for special memory such as memory-mapped hardware.

Use  std::atomic :

#include <atomic>
#include <thread>

std::atomic<bool> running{true};

void worker() {
    while (running.load()) {
        // Work
    }
}

int main() {
    std::thread t(worker);

    running.store(false);
    t.join();
}

Atomic counter

#include <atomic>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::atomic<int> counter{0};
    std::vector<std::thread> threads;

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
        threads.emplace_back([&] {
            for (int j = 0; j < 100; ++j) {
                counter.fetch_add(1);
                // counter++ is also atomic.
            }
        });
    }

    for (auto& thread : threads) {
        thread.join();
    }

    std::cout << counter.load() << '\n'; // 1000
}

Atomic operations are suitable for individual values. Use a mutex when multiple variables must satisfy one shared invariant.

Compare-and-swap

std::atomic<int> value{10};

int expected = 10;
bool changed = value.compare_exchange_strong(expected, 20);

If  value == expected , it becomes  20 . Otherwise,  expected  is updated with the actual value.

