9.  sleep()  versus  wait() 

Sleeping

std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));

Sleeping does not release mutexes already held by the thread.

Condition-variable waiting

#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>

std::mutex mutex;
std::condition_variable condition;
bool ready = false;

void waitForWork() {
    std::unique_lock lock(mutex);

    condition.wait(lock, [] {
        return ready;
    });

    // Mutex has been reacquired here.
}

void signalWork() {
    {
        std::lock_guard lock(mutex);
        ready = true;
    }

    condition.notify_one();
}

 wait() :

1. Atomically releases the mutex.
2. Suspends the thread.
3. Wakes after notification or a spurious wake-up.
4. Reacquires the mutex.
5. Rechecks the predicate.

Mappings:

condition.notify_one(); // Java notify()
condition.notify_all(); // Java notifyAll()

