11. Lock types

Normal mutex

std::mutex mutex;
std::lock_guard lock(mutex);

A normal mutex is not reentrant. Locking it twice from the same thread is invalid and may deadlock.

Reentrant lock

std::recursive_mutex mutex;
std::lock_guard lock(mutex);

Use only when recursive locking is genuinely required. Regular  std::mutex  usually produces clearer designs.

Manual/flexible lock management

std::unique_lock lock(mutex);

lock.unlock();
// Unprotected work
lock.lock();

 std::unique_lock  supports deferred locking, unlocking, moving, condition variables, and timed mutexes.

Try-lock

std::mutex mutex;

if (mutex.try_lock()) {
    try {
        // Critical section
    } catch (...) {
        mutex.unlock();
        throw;
    }
    mutex.unlock();
}

Prefer RAII after acquisition:

if (mutex.try_lock()) {
    std::lock_guard lock(mutex, std::adopt_lock);
    // Automatically unlocked
}

Timed lock

#include <chrono>
#include <mutex>

std::timed_mutex mutex;

if (mutex.try_lock_for(std::chrono::seconds(2))) {
    std::lock_guard lock(mutex, std::adopt_lock);
    // Critical section
} else {
    std::cout << "Could not acquire lock\n";
}

Locking multiple mutexes safely

std::scoped_lock lock(mutex1, mutex2);

 std::scoped_lock  uses deadlock-avoidance logic when acquiring multiple mutexes.

