13. Semaphores

C++20 supplies  <semaphore> .

Binary semaphore

#include <semaphore>

std::binary_semaphore semaphore(1);

void criticalSection() {
    semaphore.acquire();

    try {
        // Protected operation
    } catch (...) {
        semaphore.release();
        throw;
    }

    semaphore.release();
}

A mutex is normally preferable for mutual exclusion because mutex ownership is enforced.

Counting semaphore

#include <chrono>
#include <semaphore>
#include <thread>

std::counting_semaphore<3> permits(3);

void useResource() {
    permits.acquire();

    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1));

    permits.release();
}

At most three threads may pass  acquire()  simultaneously.

 release()  without a matching  acquire()  is permitted only while the count stays within the semaphore's maximum. Exceeding its maximum produces undefined behavior.

RAII permit guard

C++ semaphores do not provide a standard guard, but one is easy to create:

template<typename Semaphore>
class PermitGuard {
    Semaphore& semaphore_;

public:
    explicit PermitGuard(Semaphore& semaphore)
        : semaphore_(semaphore) {
        semaphore_.acquire();
    }

    ~PermitGuard() {
        semaphore_.release();
    }

    PermitGuard(const PermitGuard&) = delete;
    PermitGuard& operator=(const PermitGuard&) = delete;
};

