16. Scheduled execution

Run once after a delay

std::jthread scheduled([] {
    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));
    std::cout << "Executed later\n";
});

For many scheduled tasks, use Boost.Asio timers rather than creating one sleeping thread per task.

Fixed delay

std::jthread worker([](std::stop_token token) {
    while (!token.stop_requested()) {
        runTask();
        std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(2));
    }
});

The delay begins after  runTask()  finishes.

Fixed rate

std::jthread worker([](std::stop_token token) {
    using clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
    auto next = clock::now();

    while (!token.stop_requested()) {
        next += std::chrono::seconds(2);
        runTask();
        std::this_thread::sleep_until(next);
    }
});

This targets fixed start times. If execution takes too long, the schedule falls behind; overlapping requires explicitly dispatching each invocation to separate workers.

