17. Concurrent collections

The standard C++ library does not provide direct equivalents of most Java concurrent collections.

ConcurrentHashMap

Typical implementation:

#include <shared_mutex>
#include <unordered_map>

template<typename Key, typename Value>
class ConcurrentMap {
    std::unordered_map<Key, Value> map_;
    mutable std::shared_mutex mutex_;

public:
    void put(Key key, Value value) {
        std::unique_lock lock(mutex_);
        map_[std::move(key)] = std::move(value);
    }

    bool get(const Key& key, Value& result) const {
        std::shared_lock lock(mutex_);

        auto it = map_.find(key);
        if (it == map_.end()) {
            return false;
        }

        result = it->second;
        return true;
    }
};

This uses one read-write lock for the entire map. For high scalability, use:

•  oneapi::tbb::concurrent_hash_map 
•  folly::ConcurrentHashMap 
• A sharded map with multiple mutexes

Copy-on-write list

Use an immutable snapshot through  shared_ptr :

#include <atomic>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

class CopyOnWriteList {
    std::atomic<std::shared_ptr<const std::vector<int>>> data_{
        std::make_shared<const std::vector<int>>()
    };

public:
    void add(int value) {
        auto oldData = data_.load();

        while (true) {
            auto copy = std::make_shared<std::vector<int>>(*oldData);
            copy->push_back(value);

            std::shared_ptr<const std::vector<int>> newData = copy;

            if (data_.compare_exchange_weak(oldData, newData)) {
                return;
            }
        }
    }

    auto snapshot() const {
        return data_.load();
    }
};

Readers retain a stable snapshot. Writes copy the entire vector.

Concurrent queue

There is no standard lock-free concurrent queue. Use:

•  oneapi::tbb::concurrent_queue 
•  boost::lockfree::queue 
• Folly queues
• A mutex-protected  std::queue 
• The blocking queue implementation shown earlier

Implementing a lock-free linked queue manually is difficult because correct memory reclamation requires techniques such as hazard pointers or epoch-based reclamation.

Priority blocking queue

Combine:

std::priority_queue<T>
std::mutex
std::condition_variable

Direct hand-off / synchronous queue

This is usually implemented using a mutex, condition variables, and one optional value. There is no direct standard class.

Iterator behavior

C++ generally does not define Java-style fail-fast iterators. Concurrent modification of a standard container without synchronization is usually a data race and undefined behavior, not a guaranteed exception.

Use one of these strategies:

• Hold a lock during iteration.
• Copy a snapshot while locked, then iterate over the copy.
• Use an immutable copy-on-write snapshot.
• Use a concurrent container with documented iteration semantics.

